{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\" Check if in given list of numbers, are any two numbers closer to each other than\n    given threshold.\n    >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 2.0, 3.0], 0.5)\n    False\n    >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 2.8, 3.0, 4.0, 5.0, 2.0], 0.3)\n    True\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(numbers)\n    if n < 2:\n        return False\n\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)  # Sorting helps optimize comparison.\n\n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):  # Iterate through all distinct pairs.\n            if abs(sorted_numbers[i] - sorted_numbers[j]) < threshold:\n                return True\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\nclass EmptyInputError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception raised when an input list is empty.\n    It indicates that a non-empty list is required for the operation.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str = \"Input list cannot be empty.\"):\n        super().__init__(message)\n\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD) of a list of numbers around their mean.\n    MAD is the average of the absolute differences between each data point and the dataset's mean.\"\"\"\n\n    if not numbers:\n        raise EmptyInputError(\"Input list for MAD calculation cannot be empty.\")\n    \n    if len(numbers) == 1:\n        return 0.0\n\n    mean_value = sum(numbers) / len(numbers)\n    absolute_differences = [abs(x - mean_value) for x in numbers]\n    return sum(absolute_differences) / len(numbers)\n```"}
