{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"Returns a list of prime factors for the given integer `n`, sorted ascendingly.\n    Each factor appears as many times as it divides the original number, and their product equals `n`.\n    >>> factorize(8)\n    [2, 2, 2]\n    >>> factorize(25)\n    [5, 5]\n    >>> factorize(70)\n    [2, 5, 7]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    factors = []\n    d = 2\n    temp_n = n\n    while d * d <= temp_n:\n        while temp_n % d == 0:\n            factors.append(d)\n            temp_n //= d\n        d += 1\n    if temp_n > 1:\n        factors.append(temp_n)\n    return factors\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/26", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\nfrom collections import Counter\n\n\ndef remove_duplicates(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" From a list of integers, remove all elements that occur more than once.\n    Keep order of elements left the same as in the input.\n    >>> remove_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 2, 4])\n    [1, 3, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    counts = Counter(numbers)\n    result = []\n    for num in numbers:\n        if counts[num] == 1:\n            result.append(num)\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"Return a list of prime factors for a given integer n, ordered smallest to largest, including correct multiplicities.\n    The product of these factors will equal n.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    if n == 1:\n        return []\n\n    factors = []\n    d = 2\n    temp_n = n\n\n    while d * d <= temp_n:\n        while temp_n % d == 0:\n            factors.append(d)\n            temp_n //= d\n        d += 1\n\n    if temp_n > 1:\n        factors.append(temp_n)\n\n    return factors\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\" For a given number n, find the largest number that divides n evenly, smaller than n\n    >>> largest_divisor(15)\n    5\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer greater than 1.\")\n\n    # Iterate downwards from n // 2 to 1.\n    # The first number that divides n evenly will be the largest divisor smaller than n.\n    for i in range(n // 2, 0, -1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return i\n    # If the loop completes, n is prime.\n    # In this case, 1 is its largest proper divisor.\n    return 1\n```"}
