{"task_id": "HumanEval/69", "solution": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef search(lst):\n    '''\n    You are given a non-empty list of positive integers. Return the greatest integer that is greater than \n    zero, and has a frequency greater than or equal to the value of the integer itself. \n    The frequency of an integer is the number of times it appears in the list.\n    If no such a value exist, return -1.\n    Examples:\n        search([4, 1, 2, 2, 3, 1]) == 2\n        search([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4, 4]) == 3\n        search([5, 5, 4, 4, 4]) == -1\n    '''\n    # Calculate the frequency of each integer in the list.\n    counts = Counter(lst)\n    \n    candidates = []\n    # Identify integers where frequency is greater than or equal to their value.\n    for num, freq in counts.items():\n        if freq >= num:\n            candidates.append(num)\n    \n    # Return the greatest qualifying integer or -1 if none exist.\n    if not candidates:\n        return -1\n    \n    return max(candidates)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/69", "solution": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef search(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the greatest positive integer whose frequency is greater than or equal to its value.\n    Returns -1 if no such integer exists.\n    \"\"\"\n    frequency_map = Counter(lst)\n\n    candidates = []\n    for num, freq in frequency_map.items():\n        if freq >= num:\n            candidates.append(num)\n\n    if not candidates:\n        return -1\n\n    return max(candidates)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/64", "solution": "```python\ndef vowels_count(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of vowels in a string, where 'y' is a vowel only if it's at the end.\n    Vowels are 'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u' (case-insensitive).\n    \"\"\"\n    vowel_count = 0\n    s_lower = s.lower() # Convert the entire string to lowercase for case-insensitive comparison\n    \n    for i, char in enumerate(s_lower):\n        if char in \"aeiou\":\n            vowel_count += 1\n        # 'y' is considered a vowel only when it is at the end of the string.\n        elif char == 'y' and i == len(s_lower) - 1:\n            vowel_count += 1\n            \n    return vowel_count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/70", "solution": "```python\nimport collections\n\ndef strange_sort_list(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts a list of integers by repeatedly taking the minimum and then the maximum of the remaining elements.\n    This creates a new list in the specified alternating min-max order.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n\n    # Sort the list ascending and convert to a deque for efficient popping from both ends.\n    sorted_elements = collections.deque(sorted(lst))\n    result = []\n    is_min_turn = True\n\n    while sorted_elements:\n        if is_min_turn:\n            result.append(sorted_elements.popleft())  # Remove and append the smallest element\n        else:\n            result.append(sorted_elements.pop())      # Remove and append the largest remaining element\n        is_min_turn = not is_min_turn\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/71", "solution": "```python\ndef triangle_area(a, b, c):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the area of a triangle given its side lengths, rounded to 2 decimal points. Returns -1 for invalid triangles.\"\"\"\n    import math\n\n    # Validate if side lengths are positive and satisfy the triangle inequality.\n    if not (a > 0 and b > 0 and c > 0) or not ((a + b > c) and (a + c > b) and (b + c > a)):\n        return -1.0\n\n    # Calculate the semi-perimeter.\n    s = (a + b + c) / 2\n    # Calculate the area squared using Heron's formula.\n    area_squared = s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c)\n\n    # Handle potential floating point inaccuracies that might make area_squared slightly negative.\n    if area_squared < 0:\n        return 0.0\n\n    # Calculate the square root to get the raw area and round to two decimal places.\n    raw_area = math.sqrt(area_squared)\n    return round(raw_area, 2)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/72", "solution": "```python\ndef will_it_fly(q,w):\n    '''\n    Write a function that returns True if the object q will fly, and False otherwise.\n    The object q will fly if it's balanced (it is a palindromic list) and the sum of its elements is less than or equal the maximum possible weight w.\n\n    Example:\n    will_it_fly([1, 2], 5) \u279e False \n    # 1+2 is less than the maximum possible weight, but it's unbalanced.\n\n    will_it_fly([3, 2, 3], 1) \u279e False\n    # it's balanced, but 3+2+3 is more than the maximum possible weight.\n\n    will_it_fly([3, 2, 3], 9) \u279e True\n    # 3+2+3 is less than the maximum possible weight, and it's balanced.\n\n    will_it_fly([3], 5) \u279e True\n    # 3 is less than the maximum possible weight, and it's balanced.\n    '''\n    is_balanced = (q == q[::-1])\n    sum_elements = sum(q)\n\n    if is_balanced and sum_elements <= w:\n        return True\n    else:\n        return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/70", "solution": "```python\ndef strange_sort_list(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts a list of integers in a specific \\\"strange\\\" order (min, max, min, max...).\n    It starts with the minimum value, then the maximum of remaining, then minimum of remaining, and so on.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(lst, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n    if not all(isinstance(x, int) for x in lst):\n        raise TypeError(\"All elements in the list must be integers.\")\n\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n\n    temp_list = sorted(lst)\n    result = []\n    is_min_turn = True\n\n    while temp_list:\n        if is_min_turn:\n            element = temp_list.pop(0)\n        else:\n            element = temp_list.pop()\n        result.append(element)\n        is_min_turn = not is_min_turn\n            \n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/72", "solution": "```python\ndef is_palindromic(q: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a list is palindromic.\n    An empty list is considered palindromic.\n    \"\"\"\n    return q == q[::-1]\n\ndef sum_list_elements(q: list) -> int | float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of all numeric elements in a list.\n    Raises TypeError if non-numeric elements are present.\n    \"\"\"\n    return sum(q)\n\ndef will_it_fly(q: list, w: int | float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns True if the list q is palindromic and the sum of its elements\n    is less than or equal to w. Otherwise, returns False.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(q, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'q' must be a list.\")\n    if not isinstance(w, (int, float)):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'w' must be an integer or a float.\")\n\n    is_palindrome = is_palindromic(q)\n    list_sum = sum_list_elements(q)\n\n    return is_palindrome and (list_sum <= w)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/73", "solution": "```python\ndef smallest_change(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the minimum number of changes to make an array palindromic.\n    It compares elements from both ends, incrementing a counter for mismatches.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(arr, (list, tuple)):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'arr' must be a list or tuple of integers.\")\n\n    for item in arr:\n        if not isinstance(item, int):\n            raise ValueError(\"All elements in 'arr' must be integers.\")\n\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 0\n\n    changes = 0\n    left = 0\n    right = n - 1\n\n    while left < right:\n        if arr[left] != arr[right]:\n            changes += 1\n        left += 1\n        right -= 1\n    return changes\n```"}
