{"task_id": "HumanEval/71", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\nINVALID_TRIANGLE_AREA = -1.0\n\ndef _round_to_two_decimals(value: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Rounds a float value to two decimal places.\"\"\"\n    return round(value, 2)\n\ndef _is_valid_triangle(a: float, b: float, c: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if three sides form a valid triangle. All sides must be positive and obey the triangle inequality theorem.\"\"\"\n    if a <= 0 or b <= 0 or c <= 0:\n        return False\n    return (a + b > c) and (a + c > b) and (b + c > a)\n\ndef _herons_formula(a: float, b: float, c: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the area using Heron's formula. This method assumes the input sides form a valid triangle.\"\"\"\n    s = (a + b + c) / 2\n    # Use max(0, term) to handle potential floating-point inaccuracies near degenerate cases.\n    term = s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c)\n    return math.sqrt(max(0, term))\n\ndef triangle_area(a: float, b: float, c: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the area of a triangle given its three side lengths. Returns the area rounded to 2 decimal places, or -1 for an invalid triangle.\"\"\"\n    if not _is_valid_triangle(a, b, c):\n        return INVALID_TRIANGLE_AREA\n\n    raw_area = _herons_formula(a, b, c)\n    return _round_to_two_decimals(raw_area)\n```"}
