{"task_id": "HumanEval/85", "solution": "```python\ndef add(lst: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Given a non-empty list of integers, add the even elements that are at odd indices.\n    Raises TypeError or ValueError for invalid inputs.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(lst, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n    if not lst:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list cannot be empty.\")\n\n    total_sum = 0\n    for i, element in enumerate(lst):\n        if not isinstance(element, int):\n            raise TypeError(f\"List element at index {i} is not an integer: {element}\")\n        if i % 2 != 0 and element % 2 == 0:  # Check for odd index and even element\n            total_sum += element\n    return total_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/86", "solution": "```python\nimport re\n\ndef anti_shuffle(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a string where characters within each word are sorted by ASCII value.\n    Maintains original word order and spacing.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not s:\n        return \"\"\n\n    # Split the string by one or more spaces, capturing the spaces themselves\n    parts = re.split(r'(\\s+)', s)\n    \n    result_parts = []\n    for part in parts:\n        # If the part is entirely spaces (or empty string from split), keep it as is\n        if not part or part.strip() == \"\":\n            result_parts.append(part)\n        else: # Otherwise, it's a word; sort its characters\n            result_parts.append(\"\".join(sorted(part)))\n    \n    return \"\".join(result_parts)\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/87", "solution": "```python\ndef get_row(lst, x):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds integers x in a 2D ragged list and returns a list of (row, column)\n    coordinates, sorted by row ascending, then by column descending.\n    \"\"\"\n    coordinates = []\n    for r_idx, row in enumerate(lst):\n        for c_idx, val in enumerate(row):\n            if val == x:\n                coordinates.append((r_idx, c_idx))\n    \n    # Sort by row ascending, then column descending.\n    # The negative of c_idx is used for descending order.\n    coordinates.sort(key=lambda coord: (coord[0], -coord[1]))\n    return coordinates\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/88", "solution": "def sort_array(array):\n    \"\"\"Sorts an array of non-negative integers based on the parity of the sum of its first and last elements. Returns a new sorted array, leaving the original array unchanged.\"\"\"\n\n    copied_array = list(array)  # Create a copy to ensure the original array remains unmodified.\n\n    # Handle empty or single-element arrays as per FR4 and FR5.\n    if len(copied_array) < 2:\n        return copied_array\n\n    # Calculate sum of first and last elements for sorting condition.\n    first_element = copied_array[0]\n    last_element = copied_array[-1]\n    sum_first_last = first_element + last_element\n\n    # Apply sorting logic based on the sum's parity (FR1 and FR2).\n    if sum_first_last % 2 != 0:  # Sum is odd, sort in ascending order.\n        return sorted(copied_array)\n    else:  # Sum is even, sort in descending order.\n        return sorted(copied_array, reverse=True)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/89", "solution": "```python\ndef encrypt(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Encrypts the input string using a Caesar cipher, rotating alphabetic\n    characters by 4 places and preserving case. Non-alphabetic characters\n    remain unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(s, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a string.\")\n\n    encrypted_chars = []\n    rotation_value = 4  # The alphabet is rotated by 2 * 2 = 4 places.\n\n    for char in s:\n        if 'a' <= char <= 'z':\n            # Process lowercase letters.\n            base = ord('a')\n            offset = ord(char) - base\n            rotated_offset = (offset + rotation_value) % 26\n            encrypted_chars.append(chr(base + rotated_offset))\n        elif 'A' <= char <= 'Z':\n            # Process uppercase letters.\n            base = ord('A')\n            offset = ord(char) - base\n            rotated_offset = (offset + rotation_value) % 26\n            encrypted_chars.append(chr(base + rotated_offset))\n        else:\n            # Preserve non-alphabetic characters.\n            encrypted_chars.append(char)\n\n    return \"\".join(encrypted_chars)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/87", "solution": "```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\n@dataclass\nclass Coordinate:\n    row: int\n    col: int\n\n    def __lt__(self, other: 'Coordinate') -> bool:\n        # Sort by row ascending, then by column descending for same row.\n        if self.row != other.row:\n            return self.row < other.row\n        return self.col > other.col\n\n    def __eq__(self, other: object) -> bool:\n        # Checks for equality based on row and column.\n        if not isinstance(other, Coordinate):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self.row == other.row and self.col == other.col\n\nclass CoordinateFinder:\n    def find_coordinates(self, lst: List[List[int]], x: int) -> List[Coordinate]:\n        # Iterates through the list to find all occurrences of x.\n        found_coords: List[Coordinate] = []\n        for r_idx, row_list in enumerate(lst):\n            if not isinstance(row_list, list):\n                continue # Skips non-list elements for robustness.\n            for c_idx, item in enumerate(row_list):\n                if item == x:\n                    found_coords.append(Coordinate(row=r_idx, col=c_idx))\n        return found_coords\n\n    def sort_coordinates(self, coordinates: List[Coordinate]) -> List[Coordinate]:\n        # Sorts a list of Coordinate objects using Coordinate's custom __lt__ method.\n        return sorted(coordinates)\n\ndef get_row(lst, x):\n    \"\"\"\n    You are given a 2 dimensional data, as a nested lists,\n    which is similar to matrix, however, unlike matrices,\n    each row may contain a different number of columns.\n    Given lst, and integer x, find integers x in the list,\n    and return list of tuples, [(x1, y1), (x2, y2) ...] such that\n    each tuple is a coordinate - (row, columns), starting with 0.\n    Sort coordinates initially by rows in ascending order.\n    Also, sort coordinates of the row by columns in descending order.\n    \n    Examples:\n    get_row([\n      [1,2,3,4,5,6],\n      [1,2,3,4,1,6],\n      [1,2,3,4,5,1]\n    ], 1) == [(0, 0), (1, 4), (1, 0), (2, 5), (2, 0)]\n    get_row([], 1) == []\n    get_row([[], [1], [1, 2, 3]], 3) == [(2, 2)]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(lst, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'lst' must be a list.\")\n    if not isinstance(x, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'x' must be an integer.\")\n\n    finder = CoordinateFinder()\n\n    # Find coordinates and sort them according to the specification.\n    coordinate_objects = finder.find_coordinates(lst, x)\n    sorted_coordinate_objects = finder.sort_coordinates(coordinate_objects)\n\n    # Convert Coordinate objects to (int, int) tuples for the final result.\n    result_tuples = [(coord.row, coord.col) for coord in sorted_coordinate_objects]\n\n    return result_tuples\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/88", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef sort_array(array: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"Sorts an array of non-negative integers based on the sum of its first and last elements.\n    Returns a new list, sorted ascending if the sum is odd, or descending if the sum is even.\"\"\"\n\n    # Validate input array type and elements.\n    if not isinstance(array, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n    for item in array:\n        if not isinstance(item, int):\n            raise TypeError(\"All elements in the array must be integers.\")\n        if item < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"All elements in the array must be non-negative.\")\n\n    # Handle edge cases for empty or single-element arrays.\n    if not array:\n        return []\n    if len(array) == 1:\n        return list(array) # Return a copy as required.\n\n    # Determine sorting order based on the parity of the sum of first and last elements.\n    first_element = array[0]\n    last_element = array[-1]\n    \n    # Calculate sum and check its parity.\n    sum_elements = first_element + last_element\n    is_sum_odd = (sum_elements % 2 != 0)\n\n    # Perform sorting and return a new sorted list.\n    if is_sum_odd:\n        return sorted(array) # Ascending order for odd sum.\n    else:\n        return sorted(array, reverse=True) # Descending order for even sum.\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/86", "solution": "```python\nimport re\nfrom typing import List\n\nclass WordProcessor:\n    \"\"\"A utility class for sorting characters within a single word.\"\"\"\n    def sort_characters(self, word: str) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Sorts a word's characters by ASCII values. Returns the word with sorted characters.\"\"\"\n        return \"\".join(sorted(word))\n\nclass AntiShuffleService:\n    \"\"\"Orchestrates the anti-shuffle operation, sorting words and reassembling the result.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, word_processor: WordProcessor):\n        \"\"\"Initializes the service with a WordProcessor instance.\"\"\"\n        self._word_processor = word_processor\n\n    def anti_shuffle(self, s: str) -> str:\n        \"\"\"\n        Processes a string by sorting characters within each word while preserving word and space order.\n        Raises TypeError for non-string input.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(s, str):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input must be a string.\")\n\n        parts: List[str] = re.split(r'(\\s+)', s)\n        \n        result_parts: List[str] = []\n        for part in parts:\n            if part.strip() == '':\n                result_parts.append(part)\n            else:\n                result_parts.append(self._word_processor.sort_characters(part))\n        \n        return \"\".join(result_parts)\n\ndef anti_shuffle(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Write a function that takes a string and returns an ordered version of it.\n    Ordered version of string, is a string where all words (separated by space)\n    are replaced by a new word where all the characters arranged in\n    ascending order based on ascii value.\n    Note: You should keep the order of words and blank spaces in the sentence.\n    \"\"\"\n    service = AntiShuffleService(WordProcessor())\n    return service.anti_shuffle(s)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/89", "solution": "```python\ndef encrypt(s):\n    \"\"\"Encrypts a string using a Caesar cipher variant.\n    Alphabetic characters are rotated by 4 positions, preserving case; non-alphabetic characters remain unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(s, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 's' must be a string.\")\n\n    encrypted_chars = []\n    shift = 4\n\n    for char in s:\n        if 'a' <= char <= 'z':\n            # Rotate lowercase alphabetic characters.\n            start = ord('a')\n            rotated_char = chr(start + (ord(char) - start + shift) % 26)\n            encrypted_chars.append(rotated_char)\n        elif 'A' <= char <= 'Z':\n            # Rotate uppercase alphabetic characters.\n            start = ord('A')\n            rotated_char = chr(start + (ord(char) - start + shift) % 26)\n            encrypted_chars.append(rotated_char)\n        else:\n            # Leave non-alphabetic characters unchanged.\n            encrypted_chars.append(char)\n            \n    return \"\".join(encrypted_chars)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/90", "solution": "```python\ndef next_smallest(lst: list[int]) -> int | None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the second smallest unique integer from a list.\n    Returns None if the list is empty or contains fewer than two unique elements.\n    \n    next_smallest([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) == 2\n    next_smallest([5, 1, 4, 3, 2]) == 2\n    next_smallest([]) == None\n    next_smallest([1, 1]) == None\n    \"\"\"\n    if not lst:\n        return None\n\n    unique_elements = sorted(list(set(lst)))\n\n    if len(unique_elements) < 2:\n        return None\n    else:\n        return unique_elements[1]\n```"}
