{"task_id": "HumanEval/110", "solution": "```python\ndef exchange(lst1: list[int], lst2: list[int]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if lst1 can be made entirely of even numbers by exchanging elements with lst2.\n    It counts odd numbers in lst1 and even numbers in lst2 to check feasibility.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    # Count the number of odd integers in lst1 that need to be replaced.\n    odd_count_lst1 = sum(1 for x in lst1 if x % 2 != 0)\n\n    # Count the number of even integers available in lst2 for exchange.\n    even_count_lst2 = sum(1 for x in lst2 if x % 2 == 0)\n\n    # If there are enough even numbers in lst2 to replace all odd numbers in lst1, it is possible.\n    if even_count_lst2 >= odd_count_lst1:\n        return \"YES\"\n    else:\n        return \"NO\"\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/109", "solution": "```python\ndef move_one_ball(arr):\n    \"\"\"We have an array 'arr' of N integers arr[1], arr[2], ..., arr[N].The\n    numbers in the array will be randomly ordered. Your task is to determine if\n    it is possible to get an array sorted in non-decreasing order by performing \n    the following operation on the given array:\n        You are allowed to perform right shift operation any number of times.\n    \n    One right shift operation means shifting all elements of the array by one\n    position in the right direction. The last element of the array will be moved to\n    the starting position in the array i.e. 0th index. \n\n    If it is possible to obtain the sorted array by performing the above operation\n    then return True else return False.\n    If the given array is empty then return True.\n\n    Note: The given list is guaranteed to have unique elements.\n\n    For Example:\n    \n    move_one_ball([3, 4, 5, 1, 2])==>True\n    Explanation: By performin 2 right shift operations, non-decreasing order can\n                 be achieved for the given array.\n    move_one_ball([3, 5, 4, 1, 2])==>False\n    Explanation:It is not possible to get non-decreasing order for the given\n                array by performing any number of right shift operations.\n                \n    \"\"\"\n\n    def is_sorted(current_arr: list[int]) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the given array is sorted in non-decreasing order.\n        An empty array is considered sorted.\"\"\"\n        if not current_arr:\n            return True\n        return all(current_arr[i] <= current_arr[i+1] for i in range(len(current_arr) - 1))\n\n    def _right_shift(current_arr: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"Performs a single right shift operation and returns a new list.\n        The original array is not modified.\"\"\"\n        if not current_arr:\n            return []\n        return [current_arr[-1]] + current_arr[:-1]\n\n    if not arr:\n        return True\n\n    n = len(arr)\n    current_arr = list(arr) # Create a mutable copy to perform shifts\n\n    # Iterate through all N possible shifts, checking for sortedness after each.\n    for _ in range(n):\n        if is_sorted(current_arr):\n            return True\n        current_arr = _right_shift(current_arr)\n    \n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/111", "solution": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\nfrom typing import Dict\n\ndef histogram(test: str) -> Dict[str, int]:\n    \"\"\"Given a string of space-separated lowercase letters, returns a dictionary\n    of the letters with the most repetitions and their counts.\n    If several letters share the highest frequency, all are included.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(test, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a string.\")\n\n    if not test:\n        return {}\n\n    # Split the input string and count frequencies, filtering out empty strings from multiple spaces.\n    frequencies = Counter(letter for letter in test.split(' ') if letter)\n\n    if not frequencies:\n        # Handles cases where input like '   ' results in no valid letters after splitting and filtering.\n        return {}\n\n    # Determine the maximum occurrence count among all letters.\n    max_count = max(frequencies.values())\n\n    # Return a dictionary containing only the letters that have the maximum occurrence count.\n    result = {\n        letter: count\n        for letter, count in frequencies.items()\n        if count == max_count\n    }\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/111", "solution": "```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef histogram(test):\n    \"\"\"Given a string representing space separated lowercase letters, return a dictionary\n    of the letter with the most repetition and containing the corresponding count.\n    If several letters have the same occurrence, return all of them.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(test, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a string.\")\n\n    if not test:\n        return {}\n\n    frequency_map = defaultdict(int)\n    # Split the input string by spaces to get individual tokens.\n    tokens = test.split(' ')\n\n    for token in tokens:\n        # Check if the token is a single lowercase alphabetic character.\n        if len(token) == 1 and token.islower() and token.isalpha():\n            frequency_map[token] += 1\n\n    if not frequency_map:\n        return {}\n\n    max_count = 0\n    # Find the maximum occurrence count among all letters.\n    for count in frequency_map.values():\n        if count > max_count:\n            max_count = count\n\n    result = {}\n    # Collect all letters that match the maximum occurrence count.\n    for letter, count in frequency_map.items():\n        if count == max_count:\n            result[letter] = count\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/112", "solution": "```python\ndef reverse_delete(s, c):\n    \"\"\"Task\n    We are given two strings s and c, you have to deleted all the characters in s that are equal to any character in c\n    then check if the result string is palindrome.\n    A string is called palindrome if it reads the same backward as forward.\n    You should return a tuple containing the result string and True/False for the check.\n    Example\n    For s = \"abcde\", c = \"ae\", the result should be ('bcd',False)\n    For s = \"abcdef\", c = \"b\"  the result should be ('acdef',False)\n    For s = \"abcdedcba\", c = \"ab\", the result should be ('cdedc',True)\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Helper function to remove characters from a string.\n    def _filter_string(source_s: str, chars_to_remove: str) -> str:\n        return \"\".join(char for char in source_s if char not in chars_to_remove)\n\n    # Helper function to check if a string is a palindrome.\n    def _is_palindrome(check_s: str) -> bool:\n        return check_s == check_s[::-1]\n\n    filtered_s = _filter_string(s, c)\n    is_pal = _is_palindrome(filtered_s)\n    return (filtered_s, is_pal)\n\n```"}
