{"task_id": "HumanEval/124", "solution": "def valid_date(date):\n    \"\"\"Validates a given date string according to specified rules.\n    Returns True if the date string is valid, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not date:\n        return False\n\n    # Check FR1: The date string is not empty. (Already handled by `if not date:`)\n    \n    # FR2: The system shall validate that the date string adheres to the `mm-dd-yyyy` format.\n    parts = date.split('-')\n    if len(parts) != 3:\n        return False\n\n    # Ensure all parts are numeric and have correct lengths (mm=2, dd=2, yyyy=4).\n    if not (parts[0].isdigit() and parts[1].isdigit() and parts[2].isdigit()):\n        return False\n    if not (len(parts[0]) == 2 and len(parts[1]) == 2 and len(parts[2]) == 4):\n        return False\n\n    try:\n        month = int(parts[0])\n        day = int(parts[1])\n        # year = int(parts[2]) # The year component is not used for specific validation rules.\n    except ValueError:\n        # This case should be largely covered by isdigit() checks, but is a safeguard.\n        return False\n\n    # FR3: The system shall validate that the month component is between 1 and 12, inclusive.\n    if not (1 <= month <= 12):\n        return False\n\n    # FR4: The system shall validate that the day component is within the correct range for its corresponding month.\n    DAYS_IN_MONTH = {\n        1: 31, 3: 31, 5: 31, 7: 31, 8: 31, 10: 31, 12: 31,\n        4: 30, 6: 30, 9: 30, 11: 30,\n        2: 29  # February has a maximum of 29 days, as per the explicit requirements.\n    }\n\n    if not (1 <= day): # The day must be at least 1.\n        return False\n\n    max_days = DAYS_IN_MONTH.get(month)\n    # This scenario should not occur if month validation passes, but `get()` handles unknown months safely.\n    if max_days is None:\n        return False \n    \n    if not (day <= max_days):\n        return False\n\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/126", "solution": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef is_sorted(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a list is sorted in ascending order and has no number appearing three or more times.\n    Assumes non-negative integers as input.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _is_ascending(current_list):\n        \"\"\"Checks if the list is sorted in ascending order. Handles empty and single-element lists as sorted.\"\"\"\n        if len(current_list) <= 1:\n            return True\n        for i in range(len(current_list) - 1):\n            if current_list[i] > current_list[i+1]:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    def _has_excessive_duplicates(current_list):\n        \"\"\"Checks if any number in the list appears three or more times. It uses collections.Counter for efficient counting.\"\"\"\n        counts = Counter(current_list)\n        for count in counts.values():\n            if count >= 3:\n                return True\n        return False\n\n    is_ascending_result = _is_ascending(lst)\n    has_excessive_duplicates_result = _has_excessive_duplicates(lst)\n\n    return is_ascending_result and not has_excessive_duplicates_result\n```\n"}
